WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.500 --> 00:00:09.010
Welcome to Hot Topics
in Computing at MIT.

00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:11.110
I'm Dan Huttenlocher, the
dean of the Schwarzman

00:00:11.110 --> 00:00:13.270
College of Computing.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:16.090
We're really delighted to
have Brad Smith, the president

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.390
and also the chief legal
officer at Microsoft.

00:00:20.390 --> 00:00:24.440
While many of us see technology
as a double edged sword,

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:27.320
as technologists we often
tend to see the opportunity

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.740
and the potential for
good in new technologies,

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:34.150
maybe blinding us to the
other edge of the sword.

00:00:34.150 --> 00:00:36.550
Brad really stands
out in his ability

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:39.790
to articulate the balance of
positive and negative impacts

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:42.670
from technologies and how
to get better outcomes

00:00:42.670 --> 00:00:47.880
overall from understanding and
addressing these in advance.

00:00:47.880 --> 00:00:49.920
He's really transformed
how Microsoft

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.590
and much of the tech industry
see computing technology.

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.590
Growing out of the
Department of Justice actions

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.480
against Microsoft
in the 1990s, he

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:01.830
began framing the challenges
as well as the opportunities

00:01:01.830 --> 00:01:05.220
of new technologies in ways
that technology leaders could

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:07.130
appreciate.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.590
He successfully
brought Bill Gates,

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:12.200
one of the great technology
leaders of the 20th century,

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.320
on this journey, and
I would say instilling

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:17.990
in him and the company
the important roles

00:01:17.990 --> 00:01:20.180
of many societal and
government actors

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:23.160
as we think about technology.

00:01:23.160 --> 00:01:27.277
I think that we have a
lot to learn from Brad.

00:01:27.277 --> 00:01:28.860
While I know that
we're going to learn

00:01:28.860 --> 00:01:31.470
a lot in today's
fireside chat, I also

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:35.430
really highly recommend his
recent book, Tools and Weapons.

00:01:35.430 --> 00:01:37.440
It has compelling,
even, I would say,

00:01:37.440 --> 00:01:40.170
page turning vignettes,
each of which

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:43.890
makes deeper and important
points about technology

00:01:43.890 --> 00:01:46.610
and its positive and
negative impacts.

00:01:46.610 --> 00:01:49.340
We have about 250
questions that participants

00:01:49.340 --> 00:01:51.760
submitted in advance.

00:01:51.760 --> 00:01:54.260
We'll be getting to some of the
more frequent ones of those.

00:01:54.260 --> 00:01:58.820
And you can also enter questions
at the bottom of your screen.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.380
Brad is going to be
joined by Daniela

00:02:00.380 --> 00:02:02.750
Rus in this fireside chat.

00:02:02.750 --> 00:02:04.310
She's the deputy
dean of academics

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:08.880
here in the Schwarzman College
and the director of CSAIL.

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:12.060
I'm sure we're in for
a great conversation.

00:02:12.060 --> 00:02:13.740
Daniela and Brad.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:15.150
Thank you, Dan.

00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.550
We are so excited to
welcome Brad Smith

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.920
to our virtual campus today.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:23.760
Our teams first discussed
this event in the winter.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:28.380
And this feels so
long ago by now.

00:02:28.380 --> 00:02:31.230
Because Brad, there's
been so much happening

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.810
in these past few
months that has

00:02:33.810 --> 00:02:37.050
had a big imprint on
technology, so I'm really

00:02:37.050 --> 00:02:40.020
eager to get into
this conversation.

00:02:40.020 --> 00:02:42.600
We have many MIT
students with us today.

00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:45.370
And they submitted
over 200 questions.

00:02:45.370 --> 00:02:47.640
So let's get started.

00:02:47.640 --> 00:02:51.060
Now first, I saw that
your CEO, Satya Nadella,

00:02:51.060 --> 00:02:54.960
said that Microsoft
has seen two years

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:58.140
worth of digital
transformation in two months.

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:01.380
And all sorts of
organizations are working

00:03:01.380 --> 00:03:03.720
on COVID-related research.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:06.240
I'd like to start
by asking you, what

00:03:06.240 --> 00:03:08.970
do you view as the
biggest challenges

00:03:08.970 --> 00:03:13.840
Microsoft has faced so far and
what lessons have you learned?

00:03:13.840 --> 00:03:15.600
Well, first, thank you, Daniela.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:16.860
Thank you, Dan.

00:03:16.860 --> 00:03:20.050
And thanks to everybody
for watching this.

00:03:20.050 --> 00:03:23.400
I do want to just open
by saying how much we

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:26.040
at Microsoft appreciate the
opportunity to work with all

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.740
of you at MIT and,
frankly, how much we

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.870
learn from working with of you.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:33.390
It's such an important
relationship and partnership

00:03:33.390 --> 00:03:34.620
for us.

00:03:34.620 --> 00:03:36.990
Eric Horvitz leads
a lot of the work

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.930
here to work with you all,
including on the issues

00:03:39.930 --> 00:03:42.840
that we'll be talking about
today around trustworthy

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.210
and ethical artificial
intelligence

00:03:45.210 --> 00:03:47.280
and other technology.

00:03:47.280 --> 00:03:51.150
But I do think-- look, let's
start with the obvious.

00:03:51.150 --> 00:03:54.540
This is a time unlike
any in our lifetime.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:57.800
You literally have
to go back 102 years

00:03:57.800 --> 00:04:04.080
to find a pandemic that has had
the reach that COVID-19 has.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.610
I think it's
interesting to think

00:04:05.610 --> 00:04:09.810
about how different the world's
response is in the year 2020,

00:04:09.810 --> 00:04:13.590
not just compared to 1918,
but just imagine if this

00:04:13.590 --> 00:04:17.730
had happened in 2010 or 2005.

00:04:17.730 --> 00:04:21.120
In so many ways the
world has gone digital.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.770
And this event itself, I
think, really illustrates that.

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.630
You know, interestingly
for us at Microsoft,

00:04:27.630 --> 00:04:30.120
having our own
people work from home

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:33.240
has not been that
difficult. Trevor Noah

00:04:33.240 --> 00:04:35.790
joked with me-- we work
together on a number of things--

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:39.180
that just we were sort of
practicing for this every day

00:04:39.180 --> 00:04:43.110
just by being Microsoft, by
developing this technology

00:04:43.110 --> 00:04:45.460
that we use every day.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:47.760
But our role throughout
this has really

00:04:47.760 --> 00:04:51.900
been to support all of the other
organizations and institutions,

00:04:51.900 --> 00:04:54.930
governments, nonprofits,
businesses, literally

00:04:54.930 --> 00:04:58.890
on a global scale that
have had to transition

00:04:58.890 --> 00:05:01.690
to so much more remote work.

00:05:01.690 --> 00:05:03.240
And one of the
interesting things

00:05:03.240 --> 00:05:05.070
you realize, and
I think it speaks

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:08.940
to sort of the multifaceted
aspects of technology

00:05:08.940 --> 00:05:12.390
that we're talking about today,
is that of course you start

00:05:12.390 --> 00:05:14.520
with the technology itself.

00:05:14.520 --> 00:05:16.530
What is it that
people want to deploy?

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.580
Is it Teams?

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.100
Or is it more cloud services?

00:05:20.100 --> 00:05:22.410
That is sort of a
natural starting point.

00:05:22.410 --> 00:05:24.630
But then you immediately
realize that you

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:28.590
can't deploy that without
fundamentally changing business

00:05:28.590 --> 00:05:31.530
processes, government processes.

00:05:31.530 --> 00:05:34.320
We've seen the Ministry
of Justice in Italy

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:37.680
take all of its trials online.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:40.680
We've seen so many
companies take so many

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:44.220
of their business
processes, customer delivery

00:05:44.220 --> 00:05:46.950
processes online.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:49.890
And so we have to work
hand-in-hand with them.

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.540
We're not a company
that can just say,

00:05:51.540 --> 00:05:55.380
here's the tech, good luck,
let us know how it turns out.

00:05:55.380 --> 00:05:58.920
We work with them to then
customize their solutions that

00:05:58.920 --> 00:06:00.900
work on top of our platform.

00:06:00.900 --> 00:06:02.460
And then there's
one final aspect

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.950
that I think has
been so interesting.

00:06:04.950 --> 00:06:07.980
It's the old adage that
you need a clear strategy,

00:06:07.980 --> 00:06:12.240
but ultimately, culture
eats strategy for breakfast.

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:13.920
I think this has
been illustrated

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.140
really interestingly
in our schools,

00:06:16.140 --> 00:06:19.590
including our public schools
in the United States.

00:06:19.590 --> 00:06:22.590
I think they've
struggled in many states.

00:06:22.590 --> 00:06:25.890
They don't have the connectivity
for students at home.

00:06:25.890 --> 00:06:27.540
They don't have the devices.

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.600
But even when they
do all of that,

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.030
you really realize you
have to work with teachers.

00:06:33.030 --> 00:06:36.570
They need to learn not just
how to use the technology

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:39.750
but to create a
culture for a classroom

00:06:39.750 --> 00:06:42.210
when the students
aren't in the room.

00:06:42.210 --> 00:06:46.290
And so it's not just a
huge technology challenge,

00:06:46.290 --> 00:06:48.910
it's an enormous
human challenge.

00:06:48.910 --> 00:06:50.460
And I think the
great opportunity

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:54.300
for us has been the
ability to just stand next

00:06:54.300 --> 00:06:58.290
to our customers and sort of
walk a mile in their shoes

00:06:58.290 --> 00:07:00.580
so that we can try to
explore all of this together.

00:07:00.580 --> 00:07:02.880
You know, that's been just
an extraordinary opportunity

00:07:02.880 --> 00:07:04.690
for learning for us.

00:07:04.690 --> 00:07:06.870
Are there particular
efforts you have

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.600
been most proud of with
respect to this work?

00:07:11.600 --> 00:07:16.610
Well, I think in the short run,
this is a public health crisis.

00:07:16.610 --> 00:07:18.910
It's a public health
crisis that has created

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:21.640
a profound economic crisis.

00:07:21.640 --> 00:07:23.770
But I'd start with
public health.

00:07:23.770 --> 00:07:27.070
I think one of the
things that has emerged

00:07:27.070 --> 00:07:31.840
is that data has become the
indispensable tool to address

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:34.480
the public health
aspects of this.

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:37.270
Think about the
decisions that governors

00:07:37.270 --> 00:07:39.910
are making in the United
States that they've never

00:07:39.910 --> 00:07:43.660
impacted our life even
remotely as much as they

00:07:43.660 --> 00:07:45.220
have in recent months.

00:07:45.220 --> 00:07:47.200
They're running the economy.

00:07:47.200 --> 00:07:49.060
They're deciding who
can leave their house

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:51.370
and who needs to stay home.

00:07:51.370 --> 00:07:54.910
But they are decisions that
need to be based on data, data

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:57.250
about the spread
of the infection,

00:07:57.250 --> 00:08:00.850
data about hospital
capacity, data

00:08:00.850 --> 00:08:03.610
about different
counties in the state

00:08:03.610 --> 00:08:08.090
and the mortality that is
being seen and the like.

00:08:08.090 --> 00:08:12.610
And so we've worked very closely
with the counties and states

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:14.620
in the United States,
with national governments,

00:08:14.620 --> 00:08:17.170
and with the World
Health Organization

00:08:17.170 --> 00:08:21.290
to really transform the way
they are collecting data,

00:08:21.290 --> 00:08:23.692
the way they're reporting it,
the way they're sharing it

00:08:23.692 --> 00:08:25.150
with other leaders,
the way they're

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:27.290
sharing it with the public.

00:08:27.290 --> 00:08:29.590
So that has been just
sort of one of the slices

00:08:29.590 --> 00:08:31.840
I've seen in the
public health space.

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:33.909
I think another really
interesting example

00:08:33.909 --> 00:08:37.059
does come from the AI
kinds of technologies

00:08:37.059 --> 00:08:40.150
that you all at MIT have
been so involved in.

00:08:40.150 --> 00:08:45.490
It has been sort of a
moment of real importance

00:08:45.490 --> 00:08:48.130
for conversational AI.

00:08:48.130 --> 00:08:51.970
We had developed an AI, a chat
bot that was being deployed

00:08:51.970 --> 00:08:54.730
by a hospital here in Seattle.

00:08:54.730 --> 00:08:58.750
Well, it's now being deployed
by over 1,500 institutions

00:08:58.750 --> 00:09:00.430
in 23 countries.

00:09:00.430 --> 00:09:01.470
That's amazing.

00:09:01.470 --> 00:09:01.970
Yeah.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:06.340
And basically, what it does
is it allows an individual who

00:09:06.340 --> 00:09:09.160
may think they might
need a COVID-19

00:09:09.160 --> 00:09:12.370
test to go through
a conversation that

00:09:12.370 --> 00:09:14.750
will begin to determine
whether they in fact do.

00:09:14.750 --> 00:09:16.990
And if so, it can hand
off the individual

00:09:16.990 --> 00:09:21.310
to a human via
telehealth service.

00:09:21.310 --> 00:09:24.700
But in April, that was
used 190 million times.

00:09:24.700 --> 00:09:27.520
So just think about
that, that explosion.

00:09:27.520 --> 00:09:31.120
And that is, I think,
a great manifestation

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:35.200
of two years of digitization
taking place in two months.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.090
That's truly extraordinary.

00:09:37.090 --> 00:09:41.950
And now let me stay with COVID
data for a minute and ask you,

00:09:41.950 --> 00:09:44.740
so I know you've
said that you are

00:09:44.740 --> 00:09:48.580
skeptical about the
use of contact tracing.

00:09:48.580 --> 00:09:50.830
And I'm just wondering
what leads you

00:09:50.830 --> 00:09:52.780
to believe this is the case.

00:09:52.780 --> 00:09:55.510
Well, first of all,
contact tracing

00:09:55.510 --> 00:09:58.210
is of fundamental
importance in the case

00:09:58.210 --> 00:10:00.610
of any communicable disease.

00:10:00.610 --> 00:10:02.590
Now I will be
quick to say, I say

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.600
this as a person who
on New Year's Eve

00:10:04.600 --> 00:10:06.610
could have written down
perhaps everything I

00:10:06.610 --> 00:10:10.750
knew about public health on
the back of a postage stamp.

00:10:10.750 --> 00:10:14.140
This has been a crash
course for all of us.

00:10:14.140 --> 00:10:17.090
But certainly what we
learn from the present

00:10:17.090 --> 00:10:20.020
and from the past is
that for over a century

00:10:20.020 --> 00:10:23.620
whenever anybody
has a disease that

00:10:23.620 --> 00:10:26.950
is spread in an
infectious way, they're

00:10:26.950 --> 00:10:28.840
interviewed by a
public health officer.

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.670
And basically part
of the routine

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.640
is, where have you
been, who did you see.

00:10:33.640 --> 00:10:37.090
And the public health officer
then contacts, typically

00:10:37.090 --> 00:10:40.840
by phone or email, each
of those individuals.

00:10:40.840 --> 00:10:44.200
That is a process that is
fundamentally important.

00:10:44.200 --> 00:10:46.970
Now we're talking about
deploying apps for that.

00:10:46.970 --> 00:10:48.460
I think that's a good thing.

00:10:48.460 --> 00:10:49.300
Don't get me wrong.

00:10:49.300 --> 00:10:55.550
I think that app-based contact
tracing is an important tool.

00:10:55.550 --> 00:10:59.950
But I think we should recognize
that it is a tool and not

00:10:59.950 --> 00:11:01.900
a panacea.

00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:05.830
It is, in my view, sort of a
belt and suspender approach.

00:11:05.830 --> 00:11:08.410
We still will need
public health officers

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:13.150
to interview each individual who
tests positive over the phone

00:11:13.150 --> 00:11:16.750
or in person, even
if they've been using

00:11:16.750 --> 00:11:19.060
an app on their
smartphone that has,

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:23.260
say, something like
Bluetooth-based contact data

00:11:23.260 --> 00:11:24.100
on it.

00:11:24.100 --> 00:11:24.910
Why?

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:28.120
Well, the first reason
is not everybody

00:11:28.120 --> 00:11:31.450
is going to walk around
with an app on their phone.

00:11:31.450 --> 00:11:34.390
In Western Europe, in North
America, it's very clear,

00:11:34.390 --> 00:11:37.390
governments are not going
to make this mandatory.

00:11:37.390 --> 00:11:41.350
Even in Singapore,
where it's not mandatory

00:11:41.350 --> 00:11:43.540
but they have exhorted
the population

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:46.780
to use this technology,
they've struggled to get it

00:11:46.780 --> 00:11:49.990
above a 20% adoption rate.

00:11:49.990 --> 00:11:52.120
The second thing
to just remember

00:11:52.120 --> 00:11:53.620
is even if you
could get everybody

00:11:53.620 --> 00:11:58.750
who has a phone to use the
app, not everybody has a phone.

00:11:58.750 --> 00:12:02.650
Some of the most vulnerable
people in this pandemic

00:12:02.650 --> 00:12:04.310
are homeless people.

00:12:04.310 --> 00:12:07.340
There are people who are
elderly in nursing homes.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:10.510
So we just can't base our
planning on the assumption

00:12:10.510 --> 00:12:12.790
that everyone will
have this app.

00:12:12.790 --> 00:12:14.830
Finally, we need
to recognize, this

00:12:14.830 --> 00:12:16.750
is a smartphone
we're talking about.

00:12:16.750 --> 00:12:19.090
It's not an ankle bracelet.

00:12:19.090 --> 00:12:22.150
People forget to take it with
them to the grocery store.

00:12:22.150 --> 00:12:23.590
The battery dies.

00:12:23.590 --> 00:12:25.790
So I think it's
an important tool.

00:12:25.790 --> 00:12:27.820
I think it's very valuable.

00:12:27.820 --> 00:12:30.970
I think it can make
life easier and it

00:12:30.970 --> 00:12:35.180
can make this whole approach to
contact tracing more effective.

00:12:35.180 --> 00:12:37.900
But it won't replace
the humans who need

00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:40.270
to do this contact tracing.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:42.280
And frankly, it won't
replace the need

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.830
for individuals who
test positive still

00:12:44.830 --> 00:12:47.230
to go through a
lengthy interview.

00:12:47.230 --> 00:12:49.990
What about privacy with
respect to contact tracing?

00:12:49.990 --> 00:12:51.800
Is that a concern?

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:53.510
Well, I think it absolutely is.

00:12:53.510 --> 00:12:56.465
And I think it's an important
thing for us to focus on.

00:12:56.465 --> 00:12:58.840
And I think it's the kind of
thing that should call on us

00:12:58.840 --> 00:13:02.180
both narrowly and
broadly to remember,

00:13:02.180 --> 00:13:05.340
we need to address
some new problems

00:13:05.340 --> 00:13:09.580
but we don't need to throw what
I would call timeless values

00:13:09.580 --> 00:13:12.360
out the window to do so.

00:13:12.360 --> 00:13:16.860
A few weeks ago we published
seven privacy principles.

00:13:16.860 --> 00:13:18.930
And in some ways, I
think some of them

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:23.310
reflect the degree to which
you can harmonize, say,

00:13:23.310 --> 00:13:26.610
the protection of privacy
and the protection

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:30.210
of public health, and then
you can get to the issues

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.210
there where there may be some
real tension between them.

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:38.220
But for example, if we are
using this app and this data

00:13:38.220 --> 00:13:40.200
to protect public
health, I don't

00:13:40.200 --> 00:13:44.790
think it's a stretch to say then
the only proper use of the data

00:13:44.790 --> 00:13:47.010
is to protect public health.

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:50.220
And I don't think it's
a stretch to tell people

00:13:50.220 --> 00:13:52.770
that their data
will not be retained

00:13:52.770 --> 00:13:55.080
beyond the point in time
in which it's actually

00:13:55.080 --> 00:13:58.840
useful to protect public health.

00:13:58.840 --> 00:14:02.160
Those are two principles where
I think we can actually advance

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.960
both causes at the same time.

00:14:04.960 --> 00:14:08.370
There are other areas where
there are certain tensions,

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:11.640
but we believe these
can mostly be reconciled

00:14:11.640 --> 00:14:14.430
and we think we can
do this in a way that,

00:14:14.430 --> 00:14:18.430
by and large, strongly upholds
the protection of privacy.

00:14:18.430 --> 00:14:21.130
And I think that
should be our goal.

00:14:21.130 --> 00:14:24.540
So this is a great segue to
talking about your book, Tools

00:14:24.540 --> 00:14:27.870
and Weapons, which I finished
reading over the weekend

00:14:27.870 --> 00:14:30.750
and I enjoyed it very much.

00:14:30.750 --> 00:14:33.780
The book read to me like
an action adventure book

00:14:33.780 --> 00:14:35.730
with geeks as the heroes.

00:14:35.730 --> 00:14:37.930
So thank you for that.

00:14:37.930 --> 00:14:40.530
So to me, the book
summarizes your views

00:14:40.530 --> 00:14:43.370
on the roles
companies should take

00:14:43.370 --> 00:14:47.190
in having a more
moral stance on how

00:14:47.190 --> 00:14:50.580
technology is viewed and used.

00:14:50.580 --> 00:14:55.050
And the book also includes
some great historical context.

00:14:55.050 --> 00:14:58.290
I loved the
backstories for events

00:14:58.290 --> 00:15:02.340
I remember, for instance,
failing to track Daniel Pearl's

00:15:02.340 --> 00:15:07.650
kidnappers, successfully
finding Charlie Hebdo attackers,

00:15:07.650 --> 00:15:13.200
and hearing about how
your CEO, Satya Nadella,

00:15:13.200 --> 00:15:16.140
decided that the
security patches

00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:20.760
for WannaCry should be broadly
available to the public.

00:15:20.760 --> 00:15:22.560
And so my first
question is, what

00:15:22.560 --> 00:15:25.470
inspired you to write
this book and what did you

00:15:25.470 --> 00:15:28.770
want to communicate
to the public with it?

00:15:28.770 --> 00:15:31.083
Well, first of all,
thank you for reading it.

00:15:31.083 --> 00:15:33.000
I do find that when you
do an event like this,

00:15:33.000 --> 00:15:37.360
it encourages people to finish
it the weekend before we talk.

00:15:37.360 --> 00:15:38.970
So thank you.

00:15:38.970 --> 00:15:41.760
I love your description,
because I do think you're right.

00:15:41.760 --> 00:15:47.580
In our minds, geeks are the
heroes of a lot of dramas

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:51.570
that involve very
important inhuman problems.

00:15:51.570 --> 00:15:53.820
Carol Ann Browne
is my co-author.

00:15:53.820 --> 00:15:55.830
We work together at Microsoft.

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.800
And we really wrote
the book to try

00:15:58.800 --> 00:16:03.600
to reach a broader audience,
to bring these stories to life,

00:16:03.600 --> 00:16:07.470
to let people see how they have
been addressed in practice,

00:16:07.470 --> 00:16:12.300
not just at Microsoft but in
many other places as well,

00:16:12.300 --> 00:16:15.540
to draw on some
lessons from history.

00:16:15.540 --> 00:16:18.990
Because in our mind,
the tool of the book

00:16:18.990 --> 00:16:22.950
really captures the
challenge we think we face.

00:16:22.950 --> 00:16:26.490
Digital technology, I think, is
the world's most powerful tool

00:16:26.490 --> 00:16:31.500
for so many huge problems,
but it has become

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:34.380
a formidable weapon as well.

00:16:34.380 --> 00:16:38.820
And so our ultimate goal is
to broaden the conversation

00:16:38.820 --> 00:16:42.000
that we think the world needs to
have about where technology is

00:16:42.000 --> 00:16:45.270
going, how it needs
to be managed,

00:16:45.270 --> 00:16:48.150
and how it needs
to actually build

00:16:48.150 --> 00:16:53.160
on the geeks who are the heroes,
but now recognize that we're

00:16:53.160 --> 00:16:55.210
at a point where
these issues are

00:16:55.210 --> 00:16:57.580
of broad importance
for everybody.

00:16:57.580 --> 00:17:01.110
You don't have to
be a student at MIT.

00:17:01.110 --> 00:17:05.109
You don't need to have a
PhD in computer science

00:17:05.109 --> 00:17:08.010
to have an important
role to play.

00:17:08.010 --> 00:17:09.720
These issues affect all of us.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:11.579
And if nothing
else, as consumers,

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:15.430
as citizens, and as voters,
we're at a point in time

00:17:15.430 --> 00:17:19.920
where I think we all benefit
if everyone is better informed.

00:17:19.920 --> 00:17:22.920
So let's dig a
little bit deeper.

00:17:22.920 --> 00:17:25.920
In your book, you say
that the two inflection

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:31.230
points of this decade are
Cambridge Analytica and Edward

00:17:31.230 --> 00:17:33.300
Snowden's revelations.

00:17:33.300 --> 00:17:37.650
Can you elaborate what
makes these events so

00:17:37.650 --> 00:17:39.810
important in your mind?

00:17:39.810 --> 00:17:43.860
Well, if you look back at
the period from 2010 to 2020,

00:17:43.860 --> 00:17:47.100
I do think these were
two inflection points.

00:17:47.100 --> 00:17:51.413
The Snowden disclosures
happened in the summer of 2013.

00:17:51.413 --> 00:17:53.580
And what they did, more
than anything else, I think,

00:17:53.580 --> 00:17:56.820
is awaken the public, not
just in the United States,

00:17:56.820 --> 00:18:00.720
but around the world, to the
degree to which governments

00:18:00.720 --> 00:18:06.090
had access to so much data
about each and every one of us.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:12.330
And it led to a effort to
reform government surveillance

00:18:12.330 --> 00:18:15.240
and create more
protection for people.

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:18.750
One of the fascinating
moments for me

00:18:18.750 --> 00:18:21.180
is a story we share in the book.

00:18:21.180 --> 00:18:23.640
It was being in the
Roosevelt Room in the West

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:27.120
Wing of the White House at a
meeting in December of 2013

00:18:27.120 --> 00:18:30.450
with President Obama, Vice
President Biden, the White

00:18:30.450 --> 00:18:34.980
House senior staff, and 18
leading tech executives.

00:18:34.980 --> 00:18:39.120
And as each of us were pushing
President Obama to reform

00:18:39.120 --> 00:18:42.480
government surveillance, there
came this interesting moment

00:18:42.480 --> 00:18:44.430
when he looked around
the table and he said,

00:18:44.430 --> 00:18:46.950
I just want you
all to recognize,

00:18:46.950 --> 00:18:48.810
you all have more
data about people

00:18:48.810 --> 00:18:50.580
than the government does.

00:18:50.580 --> 00:18:54.798
And he said, I have a suspicion
that the guns will turn

00:18:54.798 --> 00:18:57.090
and there will be a moment
when the demands that you're

00:18:57.090 --> 00:19:01.350
placing on the government
will be placed on you as well.

00:19:01.350 --> 00:19:04.620
I thought it was a very
insightful comment at the time.

00:19:04.620 --> 00:19:07.140
I wrote it down, and I looked
around and I was struck,

00:19:07.140 --> 00:19:10.500
no one else was writing it down.

00:19:10.500 --> 00:19:11.910
And that moment came.

00:19:11.910 --> 00:19:14.130
It came-- it took
five years, which

00:19:14.130 --> 00:19:17.850
would have been, frankly, longer
than I predicted at the time.

00:19:17.850 --> 00:19:21.540
But it took 2018, and it
was Cambridge Analytica.

00:19:21.540 --> 00:19:24.450
I think for a variety of
reasons that we describe

00:19:24.450 --> 00:19:27.780
in the book, that is the moment
when then the public focused

00:19:27.780 --> 00:19:32.410
more on the amount of data that
tech companies have on them.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:36.180
And it's really a watershed
political moment, in my view,

00:19:36.180 --> 00:19:40.290
because it started to
unleash more scrutiny of tech

00:19:40.290 --> 00:19:43.440
companies, certainly
in the United States,

00:19:43.440 --> 00:19:46.740
but equally important,
perhaps even more important,

00:19:46.740 --> 00:19:49.370
on a global basis.

00:19:49.370 --> 00:19:54.290
So data bring us back to
privacy considerations.

00:19:54.290 --> 00:19:56.690
You have a wonderful
chapter in your book

00:19:56.690 --> 00:19:59.570
that presents privacy
as a human right.

00:19:59.570 --> 00:20:01.760
And you start this
chapter on privacy

00:20:01.760 --> 00:20:05.570
by describing your experience
in Berlin when you visited

00:20:05.570 --> 00:20:08.060
the headquarters of Stasi.

00:20:08.060 --> 00:20:11.780
And for me, this was a very
powerful moment of the book,

00:20:11.780 --> 00:20:15.920
since I was born in Romania
during Stasi-like times.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:18.950
You also say that this was one
of the most memorable moments

00:20:18.950 --> 00:20:20.390
of that year for you.

00:20:20.390 --> 00:20:21.170
How is this?

00:20:21.170 --> 00:20:25.820
How did this experience of
visiting the Stasi headquarters

00:20:25.820 --> 00:20:29.120
impact your views on
privacy as a human right?

00:20:29.120 --> 00:20:36.200
Well, it really was a moving
moment for me, for Carol Ann,

00:20:36.200 --> 00:20:40.160
and for others from Microsoft
who were on this visit.

00:20:40.160 --> 00:20:45.320
In what was East Berlin,
the former Stasi prison

00:20:45.320 --> 00:20:47.630
is now a museum.

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:51.800
And we had the opportunity
to walk through the prison

00:20:51.800 --> 00:20:56.890
with an individual who was
a prisoner there in 1968.

00:20:56.890 --> 00:20:59.690
You know, he's a
lot like a lot of us

00:20:59.690 --> 00:21:01.940
who are all
connected here today.

00:21:01.940 --> 00:21:04.670
In 1968, he was 24 years old.

00:21:04.670 --> 00:21:07.250
His name is
Hans-Jochen Scheidler.

00:21:07.250 --> 00:21:11.780
He was pursuing a graduate
degree in physics at the time.

00:21:11.780 --> 00:21:15.110
He was studying at the
University of Prague.

00:21:15.110 --> 00:21:19.130
And 1968 was the year
of the Prague Spring.

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:23.750
Well, he was back in Berlin when
the Warsaw Pact tanks rolled

00:21:23.750 --> 00:21:28.700
through Prague, just shut
down the liberalization

00:21:28.700 --> 00:21:30.830
that was taking place there.

00:21:30.830 --> 00:21:34.470
He and three of his friends
were very upset about this.

00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:37.460
So they printed up
these little leaflets

00:21:37.460 --> 00:21:41.180
and they handed them out
to people one evening.

00:21:41.180 --> 00:21:44.210
He was reported to the Stasi.

00:21:44.210 --> 00:21:46.250
They picked him up that night.

00:21:46.250 --> 00:21:51.680
They took him to this prison,
where for many, many months,

00:21:51.680 --> 00:21:56.490
he never saw another human being
except for the individuals who

00:21:56.490 --> 00:21:58.160
were interrogating them.

00:21:58.160 --> 00:22:02.000
He was never given the
opportunity to read anything.

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.730
He was never given the
opportunity to write.

00:22:04.730 --> 00:22:08.450
His parents had no idea
what had happened to him.

00:22:08.450 --> 00:22:10.370
But when you think
about what put him there

00:22:10.370 --> 00:22:14.540
in the first place, it was
this extraordinary degree

00:22:14.540 --> 00:22:17.870
of surveillance that
the Stasi had unleashed

00:22:17.870 --> 00:22:21.890
on the people of East Germany,
using all of the technology

00:22:21.890 --> 00:22:25.290
and hundreds of
thousands of informants.

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:29.400
And when you think about what
he did, he wrote a few lines

00:22:29.400 --> 00:22:32.410
and he handed it out to people.

00:22:32.410 --> 00:22:35.740
Well, in our day, that's
called sending a text message.

00:22:35.740 --> 00:22:38.170
It's called writing an email.

00:22:38.170 --> 00:22:42.430
And with cloud computing
and online services,

00:22:42.430 --> 00:22:46.540
we now live in a world
where if we're not careful,

00:22:46.540 --> 00:22:51.280
any government can use
the power of technology

00:22:51.280 --> 00:22:53.800
to unleash
surveillance on a scale

00:22:53.800 --> 00:22:57.380
that the Stasi could
not have imagined.

00:22:57.380 --> 00:23:02.260
And you see what can happen when
that kind of power is abused.

00:23:02.260 --> 00:23:05.080
And I think it's just
really important for us

00:23:05.080 --> 00:23:07.360
to use that as inspiration.

00:23:07.360 --> 00:23:09.880
One of the most
interesting moments for me

00:23:09.880 --> 00:23:13.060
was to share a short
video that we produced.

00:23:13.060 --> 00:23:18.280
We had a camera crew
with us on that visit.

00:23:18.280 --> 00:23:21.190
And we have an annual
sales meeting at Microsoft.

00:23:21.190 --> 00:23:23.770
It's in T-Mobile
Arena in Las Vegas.

00:23:23.770 --> 00:23:26.110
You're speaking
to 15,000 people.

00:23:26.110 --> 00:23:28.330
And as you can imagine
at a sales meeting,

00:23:28.330 --> 00:23:32.020
mostly it's, this was a great
year, we're doing really well.

00:23:32.020 --> 00:23:34.480
Everybody's so enthusiastic.

00:23:34.480 --> 00:23:35.980
And I showed that video.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:37.370
And I frankly worried.

00:23:37.370 --> 00:23:40.150
You know, I was like, well,
this is a bit of a downer.

00:23:40.150 --> 00:23:41.970
Is this going to land well?

00:23:41.970 --> 00:23:46.120
But I thought it was so
important for our employees

00:23:46.120 --> 00:23:49.360
to see that through
their own eyes.

00:23:49.360 --> 00:23:51.640
And I will frankly
always remember

00:23:51.640 --> 00:23:55.780
not just the visit to the
museum and the prison,

00:23:55.780 --> 00:24:00.430
but the standing ovation
of all of our employees.

00:24:00.430 --> 00:24:04.090
To me, I think this is the
message for everybody who's

00:24:04.090 --> 00:24:05.260
watching now.

00:24:05.260 --> 00:24:06.700
It is our time.

00:24:06.700 --> 00:24:09.880
And it is, frankly, going
to be your generation that

00:24:09.880 --> 00:24:13.960
will decide how this
technology impacts peoples'

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:15.880
rights around the world.

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:18.430
And I think we have
to learn from the past

00:24:18.430 --> 00:24:21.580
and, frankly, just
resolve together

00:24:21.580 --> 00:24:24.190
that we won't allow that
kind of thing to be repeated.

00:24:27.060 --> 00:24:29.730
Brad, that's so powerful to me.

00:24:29.730 --> 00:24:31.990
Let me follow up with
a question on where

00:24:31.990 --> 00:24:36.600
you see the role of technology
and of the legal system

00:24:36.600 --> 00:24:40.020
to ensure our privacy
as a human right?

00:24:40.020 --> 00:24:43.870
Well, I think two things
need to go together.

00:24:43.870 --> 00:24:47.640
First, just as technology
can be weaponized,

00:24:47.640 --> 00:24:52.320
as that last example showed,
it is a powerful tool

00:24:52.320 --> 00:24:55.320
for the protection
of human rights.

00:24:55.320 --> 00:24:58.452
We're using it, for example,
with George and Amal Clooney.

00:24:58.452 --> 00:25:00.660
Amal Clooney is one of the
great human rights lawyers

00:25:00.660 --> 00:25:01.510
in the world.

00:25:01.510 --> 00:25:04.540
And with the Clooney
Foundation for Justice,

00:25:04.540 --> 00:25:07.440
we've created a cloud
service and an app

00:25:07.440 --> 00:25:10.200
so that monitors to
go into courtrooms

00:25:10.200 --> 00:25:12.600
around the world,
courtrooms where

00:25:12.600 --> 00:25:17.910
journalists, or women, or
people from the LGBT community

00:25:17.910 --> 00:25:22.740
are potentially being persecuted
and then prosecuted in ways

00:25:22.740 --> 00:25:24.780
that put their rights at risk.

00:25:24.780 --> 00:25:28.380
And you can capture data, you
can share it with the world,

00:25:28.380 --> 00:25:30.210
you can ensure
that that trial is

00:25:30.210 --> 00:25:33.510
evaluated by judicial
experts in other countries.

00:25:33.510 --> 00:25:35.670
All of that is happening.

00:25:35.670 --> 00:25:40.170
That's just one small
example of all of the ways

00:25:40.170 --> 00:25:42.840
that technology can
be used, I think,

00:25:42.840 --> 00:25:46.500
to promote the protection
of human rights.

00:25:46.500 --> 00:25:50.100
But I do think at
the end of the day,

00:25:50.100 --> 00:25:53.310
there is no substitute
for protection

00:25:53.310 --> 00:25:58.140
under the law itself,
protection by judges and courts.

00:25:58.140 --> 00:26:00.810
And so a lot of what
we need to focus on

00:26:00.810 --> 00:26:04.710
is how technology and the
law can advance together

00:26:04.710 --> 00:26:06.060
to meet these broad needs.

00:26:06.060 --> 00:26:08.520
That's really one of
the other things that

00:26:08.520 --> 00:26:12.100
inspired us to write this book.

00:26:12.100 --> 00:26:12.600
Yes.

00:26:12.600 --> 00:26:14.940
And technology and
law need to understand

00:26:14.940 --> 00:26:17.580
each other's language
and capabilities in order

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:21.820
to make that path
forward together.

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:24.940
Now a related issue to
privacy is security.

00:26:24.940 --> 00:26:27.570
And you are instrumental
in guiding Microsoft

00:26:27.570 --> 00:26:31.020
toward building a strong
security foundation.

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:34.500
And you decided that
Microsoft needed

00:26:34.500 --> 00:26:38.850
to be the most secure company
and held your ground on this,

00:26:38.850 --> 00:26:42.270
which was visionary and
courageous, especially

00:26:42.270 --> 00:26:44.070
at a time when
people did not really

00:26:44.070 --> 00:26:46.830
care about the
privacy of their data

00:26:46.830 --> 00:26:51.250
and other companies were
betting on advertising.

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.380
I'm just wondering
about the back story.

00:26:53.380 --> 00:26:57.310
How did you decide on the
importance of security?

00:26:57.310 --> 00:27:01.260
Well, I think security became
so fundamentally important

00:27:01.260 --> 00:27:04.640
beginning in about
the year 2000.

00:27:04.640 --> 00:27:08.010
You know, software was designed
for the personal computer,

00:27:08.010 --> 00:27:10.710
but no one envisioned that the
personal computer would always

00:27:10.710 --> 00:27:13.420
be connected to the world.

00:27:13.420 --> 00:27:17.800
And so we recognized that
that was going to take place.

00:27:17.800 --> 00:27:20.620
And we saw the attacks
that started to take place.

00:27:20.620 --> 00:27:23.130
And so we started
to harden computers,

00:27:23.130 --> 00:27:27.660
we started to harden network
architecture and software.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:30.180
And what we really
then learned was

00:27:30.180 --> 00:27:32.310
that we needed to keep pace.

00:27:32.310 --> 00:27:34.830
And we needed to rely
not just on technology

00:27:34.830 --> 00:27:36.510
to protect people,
but we were going

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:38.670
to need to work with
governments in new ways.

00:27:38.670 --> 00:27:41.340
We were going to need to work
with the rest of the industry.

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.410
We were going to need
to advocate for stronger

00:27:43.410 --> 00:27:45.160
laws around the world.

00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:48.930
And so it was really that
combination that I think

00:27:48.930 --> 00:27:53.410
led us to put
ourselves on a journey.

00:27:53.410 --> 00:27:55.050
It's a journey that
continues today.

00:27:55.050 --> 00:27:58.810
We continue to see nation
states becoming more aggressive.

00:27:58.810 --> 00:28:01.260
And so we're having
to figure out

00:28:01.260 --> 00:28:05.370
exactly what new
initiatives we need to take.

00:28:05.370 --> 00:28:09.060
It's institutions at
MIT that, of course,

00:28:09.060 --> 00:28:11.340
play an extraordinarily
important role as well.

00:28:11.340 --> 00:28:13.560
We're all in this
together and we're all

00:28:13.560 --> 00:28:16.980
focused on acting together.

00:28:16.980 --> 00:28:19.650
Brad, with elections
coming up this fall,

00:28:19.650 --> 00:28:23.610
a lot of states and counties are
talking about the possibility

00:28:23.610 --> 00:28:25.260
of voting apps.

00:28:25.260 --> 00:28:31.062
What's your take on the
security of electronic voting?

00:28:31.062 --> 00:28:32.520
Well, I think the
interesting thing

00:28:32.520 --> 00:28:35.430
is in a world where we
take for granted security

00:28:35.430 --> 00:28:39.870
for financial transactions every
day, we're not yet in a world

00:28:39.870 --> 00:28:43.800
where we have security for
elections and voting the way

00:28:43.800 --> 00:28:45.310
we would like.

00:28:45.310 --> 00:28:48.970
And so what I think
we're going to need to do

00:28:48.970 --> 00:28:52.960
is look towards the
future, where we frankly

00:28:52.960 --> 00:28:54.220
can have online voting.

00:28:54.220 --> 00:28:55.930
Why not?

00:28:55.930 --> 00:28:59.620
But it is going to take time
to be able to deploy it well.

00:28:59.620 --> 00:29:01.780
I think one of the
things to just recognize

00:29:01.780 --> 00:29:04.930
is we engage in
financial transactions

00:29:04.930 --> 00:29:06.250
every moment of the day.

00:29:06.250 --> 00:29:08.110
There's billions every day.

00:29:08.110 --> 00:29:10.960
Voting happens infrequently.

00:29:10.960 --> 00:29:13.360
It's a powerful reminder
that just in life we

00:29:13.360 --> 00:29:16.380
get better at things
that we do frequently

00:29:16.380 --> 00:29:18.850
than things that we do rarely.

00:29:18.850 --> 00:29:21.700
But I think we need to put
ourselves on an ongoing quest

00:29:21.700 --> 00:29:24.910
to develop more secure
online voting solutions.

00:29:24.910 --> 00:29:28.480
In the meantime, what
we urgently need to do

00:29:28.480 --> 00:29:31.840
is secure the voting
systems that exist today.

00:29:31.840 --> 00:29:33.920
In so many places in
the United States,

00:29:33.920 --> 00:29:38.900
we have voting machines
that are 10 or 15 years old.

00:29:38.900 --> 00:29:40.860
And so one of the
really urgent needs,

00:29:40.860 --> 00:29:42.680
including for this
election year,

00:29:42.680 --> 00:29:45.680
is to keep working, typically
with municipalities,

00:29:45.680 --> 00:29:51.030
with counties, with states, to
harden their voting systems.

00:29:51.030 --> 00:29:53.480
Now you have a
powerful chapter on AI

00:29:53.480 --> 00:29:55.600
and democracy in your book.

00:29:55.600 --> 00:30:00.800
And you also warn about
a potential digital 9/11.

00:30:00.800 --> 00:30:04.360
What are you doing to prevent
this, meaning what is Microsoft

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:05.770
doing to prevent this?

00:30:05.770 --> 00:30:07.270
Well, first of all,
I would just say

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:09.550
if you think about what
a digital 9/11 would

00:30:09.550 --> 00:30:12.380
be, I think it's two things.

00:30:12.380 --> 00:30:15.310
One is it would be a
government attacking.

00:30:15.310 --> 00:30:20.710
Attacking, say, so that the
electrical grid goes down,

00:30:20.710 --> 00:30:21.940
hospitals go down.

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:24.730
That is what happened
in 2017 with WannaCry.

00:30:24.730 --> 00:30:27.880
The National Health Service
in the United Kingdom, a third

00:30:27.880 --> 00:30:30.310
of their hospitals went down.

00:30:30.310 --> 00:30:33.970
Cash registers, ATMs,
everything stops working.

00:30:33.970 --> 00:30:38.080
That's what happened in Ukraine
with the NotPetya attack.

00:30:38.080 --> 00:30:40.120
But ultimately I think
one of the worst attacks

00:30:40.120 --> 00:30:44.200
could be if we learned
a week after an election

00:30:44.200 --> 00:30:48.190
that voting had been tampered
with, that the voting rolls had

00:30:48.190 --> 00:30:49.690
been tampered with.

00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:53.060
I think if something
like that were to happen,

00:30:53.060 --> 00:30:56.428
then I think we'd
have a real issue.

00:30:56.428 --> 00:30:57.970
I mean, just think
what it would mean

00:30:57.970 --> 00:31:05.080
if no one had confidence to know
who had been elected president.

00:31:05.080 --> 00:31:09.490
That would be a
fundamental problem.

00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:14.440
Our entire basis for society,
I think, would be put at risk.

00:31:17.850 --> 00:31:19.450
I couldn't agree with you more.

00:31:19.450 --> 00:31:24.690
And I'm so glad that Microsoft
and many other organizations

00:31:24.690 --> 00:31:28.110
and researchers, including
a lot of researchers at MIT,

00:31:28.110 --> 00:31:31.990
are working on the
challenge of security.

00:31:31.990 --> 00:31:35.820
I'd like to turn it over
to a different topic,

00:31:35.820 --> 00:31:37.390
and that is regulation.

00:31:37.390 --> 00:31:40.050
And I was very intrigued.

00:31:40.050 --> 00:31:43.020
Because in your book,
you make an argument

00:31:43.020 --> 00:31:47.220
that I think is a bit rare
among executives of major tech

00:31:47.220 --> 00:31:48.010
companies.

00:31:48.010 --> 00:31:52.150
And that is that Silicon
Valley needs more regulation.

00:31:52.150 --> 00:31:54.540
And so I wonder,
can we get specific?

00:31:54.540 --> 00:31:57.660
In what particular areas
do you think companies

00:31:57.660 --> 00:32:01.110
like Microsoft need
more regulation?

00:32:01.110 --> 00:32:04.500
Well, first of all, I think
we should recognize broadly

00:32:04.500 --> 00:32:06.960
that a well functioning
market typically

00:32:06.960 --> 00:32:10.770
has room for lots
of innovation, lots

00:32:10.770 --> 00:32:15.570
of room for entrepreneurialship,
but a layer of regulation.

00:32:15.570 --> 00:32:18.150
We have more confidence
in the safety of food

00:32:18.150 --> 00:32:20.520
and drugs because of the FDA.

00:32:20.520 --> 00:32:23.610
We have more confidence
in the safety of our cars

00:32:23.610 --> 00:32:27.600
and aircraft because of
regulatory authorities.

00:32:27.600 --> 00:32:30.120
So when you put
things in that lens,

00:32:30.120 --> 00:32:32.520
I do believe that digital
technology has reached

00:32:32.520 --> 00:32:35.250
a point where it would
benefit from more regulation,

00:32:35.250 --> 00:32:39.000
not the kind of regulation
that would stifle creativity,

00:32:39.000 --> 00:32:41.400
but I do think to
protect privacy,

00:32:41.400 --> 00:32:44.160
I think to protect
security, I think

00:32:44.160 --> 00:32:48.240
to ensure the responsible use
of artificial intelligence.

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:49.920
That's why we've
called for regulation

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:52.390
of facial recognition
technology.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:54.450
We now have the first
example of that,

00:32:54.450 --> 00:32:56.730
a state law passed
in Washington state,

00:32:56.730 --> 00:33:00.420
I think not coincidentally,
where Microsoft is based.

00:33:00.420 --> 00:33:04.410
So we have all of
that to learn from.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:07.500
I think digital safety,
the protection of children,

00:33:07.500 --> 00:33:10.050
that's another obvious area.

00:33:10.050 --> 00:33:12.900
All of these I think
are ripe for regulation.

00:33:12.900 --> 00:33:15.540
And that's why we're seeing
more regulation in it.

00:33:15.540 --> 00:33:18.490
I will say-- you're going
to get a kick out of this--

00:33:18.490 --> 00:33:22.290
Microsoft is so good
at protecting security

00:33:22.290 --> 00:33:24.330
that my company's
policies are telling me

00:33:24.330 --> 00:33:28.040
that they're going to reboot
my computer in seven minutes.

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:29.970
So while I'm
listening to you, I am

00:33:29.970 --> 00:33:32.880
trying to find a way to disable
that, which I may or may not

00:33:32.880 --> 00:33:34.590
find in the next seven minutes.

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.980
So this will be one of the
more memorable security

00:33:37.980 --> 00:33:40.530
updates in my life if
this, in fact, happens.

00:33:40.530 --> 00:33:42.840
So let's see what we can cover.

00:33:42.840 --> 00:33:43.680
I hope not.

00:33:43.680 --> 00:33:46.990
I have not gotten to many
of the 200-plus questions.

00:33:46.990 --> 00:33:47.740
I know.

00:33:47.740 --> 00:33:52.150
But let me ask you
something about China.

00:33:52.150 --> 00:33:55.350
So Microsoft has had a
direct presence in China

00:33:55.350 --> 00:33:57.000
for a long time.

00:33:57.000 --> 00:34:00.750
Your R&amp;D initiatives
there are significant.

00:34:00.750 --> 00:34:03.540
Can you tell us a bit
about your experience there

00:34:03.540 --> 00:34:08.670
and how the current and
foreseeable political disputes

00:34:08.670 --> 00:34:12.449
between US and China are
affecting you, if at all?

00:34:12.449 --> 00:34:13.920
Yeah, no, they definitely are.

00:34:13.920 --> 00:34:15.420
I think they're
affecting all of us.

00:34:15.420 --> 00:34:20.670
I think it really has
a three-part impact.

00:34:20.670 --> 00:34:25.300
First, it's having an impact
on supply chain stability.

00:34:25.300 --> 00:34:28.500
And I think there are real
questions for the future as

00:34:28.500 --> 00:34:30.719
to the degree to which
American tech companies will

00:34:30.719 --> 00:34:35.219
be able to rely on the Chinese
supply chain for hardware.

00:34:35.219 --> 00:34:39.310
Second, it's having an impact
on access to the Chinese market.

00:34:39.310 --> 00:34:41.670
I think the Chinese
market has really

00:34:41.670 --> 00:34:46.510
provided only limited access to
most American tech companies.

00:34:46.510 --> 00:34:48.909
And that doesn't look
like it's going to change.

00:34:48.909 --> 00:34:51.929
In fact, I think it may get
harder rather than easier.

00:34:51.929 --> 00:34:55.380
And finally, I actually think
the single most important thing

00:34:55.380 --> 00:34:57.630
for us to think about,
the thing that's

00:34:57.630 --> 00:35:02.010
most important for Microsoft,
I would argue for MIT,

00:35:02.010 --> 00:35:04.740
it's the intellectual
interaction.

00:35:04.740 --> 00:35:07.560
China, as we all know, has
some of the world's greatest

00:35:07.560 --> 00:35:11.410
engineers, software
developers, academics.

00:35:11.410 --> 00:35:13.080
You know, software
is not created

00:35:13.080 --> 00:35:15.090
in only a single country.

00:35:15.090 --> 00:35:17.400
Especially with open source
techniques and the like,

00:35:17.400 --> 00:35:20.750
it's created by people
working together.

00:35:20.750 --> 00:35:23.100
And even if supply
chains change,

00:35:23.100 --> 00:35:25.860
even if access to the
market is limited,

00:35:25.860 --> 00:35:29.250
I think it's of profound
importance to the world

00:35:29.250 --> 00:35:31.860
that people be able
to learn and study

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:34.870
and research and think together.

00:35:34.870 --> 00:35:37.950
And I think preserving that,
amid these other challenges,

00:35:37.950 --> 00:35:41.580
is really an important
priority for all of us.

00:35:41.580 --> 00:35:44.310
What about a digital
iron curtain?

00:35:44.310 --> 00:35:47.280
You talk in the
chapter about China

00:35:47.280 --> 00:35:51.270
about the possibility of
a digital iron curtain.

00:35:51.270 --> 00:35:54.510
We've also heard
former Google CEO Eric

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:58.680
Schmidt talk about splintering
the internet in two,

00:35:58.680 --> 00:36:02.190
one Chinese led and
one non-Chinese led.

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:05.610
What would be the geopolitical
and economic impact

00:36:05.610 --> 00:36:06.780
of this splintering?

00:36:06.780 --> 00:36:12.540
And how do you see the creation
of this digital iron curtain?

00:36:12.540 --> 00:36:16.780
Well, I think the world has
been splintering since 2010.

00:36:16.780 --> 00:36:18.970
You know, the internet
in China is not the same

00:36:18.970 --> 00:36:21.640
as the internet in
the United States.

00:36:21.640 --> 00:36:24.220
But I think what we
now see more and more

00:36:24.220 --> 00:36:26.290
is obviously a
degree of technology

00:36:26.290 --> 00:36:29.410
competition between the
United States and China.

00:36:29.410 --> 00:36:31.840
We see public policies
in both countries

00:36:31.840 --> 00:36:35.020
focused on this competition.

00:36:35.020 --> 00:36:38.015
I think increasingly,
American companies

00:36:38.015 --> 00:36:39.640
have an advantage in
the United States,

00:36:39.640 --> 00:36:42.640
Chinese companies have
an advantage China.

00:36:42.640 --> 00:36:46.390
They compete with each other
in Europe and elsewhere.

00:36:46.390 --> 00:36:47.640
We'll see.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:50.370
It is increasingly a
bipolar technology world.

00:36:50.370 --> 00:36:53.230
That's the phrase
we use in our book.

00:36:53.230 --> 00:36:55.210
This will be, I think,
one of the defining

00:36:55.210 --> 00:36:57.820
issues for this decade.

00:36:57.820 --> 00:37:00.840
And a lot remains
unknown about how exactly

00:37:00.840 --> 00:37:01.840
this is going to unfold.

00:37:04.570 --> 00:37:07.720
We've talked about
a lot of challenges.

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:11.350
There is one big challenge
that is impacting everyone,

00:37:11.350 --> 00:37:13.690
and that is climate change.

00:37:13.690 --> 00:37:17.590
I personally believe
that our only way out

00:37:17.590 --> 00:37:20.320
of this big challenge
is to figure out

00:37:20.320 --> 00:37:23.950
how to cool the
planet by 1 degree

00:37:23.950 --> 00:37:27.640
rather than figuring out
how to not warm up by more

00:37:27.640 --> 00:37:30.340
than another 1 and 1/2 degrees.

00:37:30.340 --> 00:37:33.940
And I was inspired when
earlier this year Microsoft

00:37:33.940 --> 00:37:38.740
announced ambitious plans to
go carbon negative by 2030.

00:37:38.740 --> 00:37:42.370
Not only that, but
also to turn back

00:37:42.370 --> 00:37:45.250
the clock on all
the carbon Microsoft

00:37:45.250 --> 00:37:50.560
has produced since 1975 by
counterbalancing that number

00:37:50.560 --> 00:37:53.020
by 2050.

00:37:53.020 --> 00:37:56.530
What do you see as
the biggest challenge

00:37:56.530 --> 00:37:59.620
as you proceed with
this objective?

00:37:59.620 --> 00:38:01.110
Well, we're going
to, first of all,

00:38:01.110 --> 00:38:03.340
need a broad public
understanding and commitment

00:38:03.340 --> 00:38:04.870
to the goal.

00:38:04.870 --> 00:38:08.290
I think the goal needs to get
to be carbon zero, meaning

00:38:08.290 --> 00:38:11.620
net zero by 2050,
meaning as a planet we're

00:38:11.620 --> 00:38:14.980
removing every year as
much carbon as we emit.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:17.990
Now to do that, then two
things become obvious.

00:38:17.990 --> 00:38:22.600
We need to emit less carbon
and we need to remove more.

00:38:22.600 --> 00:38:26.590
We need a number of techniques
to emit less carbon.

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:28.870
Digital technology
plays a powerful role

00:38:28.870 --> 00:38:32.350
in our ability to measure
this, to create standards,

00:38:32.350 --> 00:38:35.140
ultimately to reinforce
this through private action

00:38:35.140 --> 00:38:37.600
and I think public
regulation in a way

00:38:37.600 --> 00:38:39.960
that will drive emissions down.

00:38:39.960 --> 00:38:43.450
But I think we also need a
massive investment, perhaps

00:38:43.450 --> 00:38:46.990
the biggest R&amp;D
challenge of the century,

00:38:46.990 --> 00:38:49.510
to develop new techniques
to remove carbon

00:38:49.510 --> 00:38:50.930
from the environment.

00:38:50.930 --> 00:38:53.440
Things like direct air
capture are so clearly

00:38:53.440 --> 00:38:57.880
in their infancy, they're
not yet cost effective.

00:38:57.880 --> 00:39:00.670
And we're going to need
just huge breakthroughs

00:39:00.670 --> 00:39:03.340
in that space over
the next three decades

00:39:03.340 --> 00:39:07.720
if we are going to achieve this
fundamental goal of protecting

00:39:07.720 --> 00:39:10.150
the planet the way we need to.

00:39:10.150 --> 00:39:12.880
And we have a tragedy
of the commons here.

00:39:12.880 --> 00:39:17.810
Many more companies have to step
up and follow Microsoft's lead

00:39:17.810 --> 00:39:18.310
on this.

00:39:18.310 --> 00:39:20.800
Because everyone
needs to pitch in,

00:39:20.800 --> 00:39:23.380
otherwise we won't get there.

00:39:23.380 --> 00:39:24.280
No, we do.

00:39:24.280 --> 00:39:27.280
And I'm just going to say
now, in case you lose me

00:39:27.280 --> 00:39:30.340
in one minute, thank you
for the 45 minutes rather

00:39:30.340 --> 00:39:31.000
than the hour.

00:39:31.000 --> 00:39:34.445
But let's hope that I
keep going with my update.

00:39:34.445 --> 00:39:36.565
It's put off a few minutes.

00:39:36.565 --> 00:39:39.970
So I'd like to switch
to some questions by--

00:39:39.970 --> 00:39:41.560
Go for it.

00:39:41.560 --> 00:39:43.400
This will be the
ultimate speed round.

00:39:43.400 --> 00:39:43.900
OK.

00:39:43.900 --> 00:39:45.280
So here's one.

00:39:45.280 --> 00:39:50.200
How can workers proactively
expand on their skills

00:39:50.200 --> 00:39:54.400
to adapt to the increasingly
digital AI-driven environment?

00:39:54.400 --> 00:39:55.840
Well, I would say two things.

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:59.350
People who are not in the
computer and data science field

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:01.690
need to master more
digital skills.

00:40:01.690 --> 00:40:04.900
But my plea to all of you,
because I think so many of you

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:08.520
probably are more in the
computer and data fields,

00:40:08.520 --> 00:40:12.820
is don't neglect the other
aspects of the liberal arts.

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:15.340
Make sure that
you're in a position

00:40:15.340 --> 00:40:18.490
to not only help drive
technology forward

00:40:18.490 --> 00:40:22.010
but to think more broadly
about the use cases,

00:40:22.010 --> 00:40:24.100
the fundamental
societal problems

00:40:24.100 --> 00:40:27.220
you can help solve,
the ethical, almost

00:40:27.220 --> 00:40:31.720
the philosophical issues, the
policy issues, the legal issues

00:40:31.720 --> 00:40:34.420
that will also need to
be addressed in order

00:40:34.420 --> 00:40:36.910
to advance technology in
a way that genuinely is

00:40:36.910 --> 00:40:39.550
going to best serve the world.

00:40:39.550 --> 00:40:42.190
I think only if
we're able to think

00:40:42.190 --> 00:40:45.700
in a more multidisciplinary
framework than we have

00:40:45.700 --> 00:40:49.300
in the past are we going to
be successful in ensuring

00:40:49.300 --> 00:40:52.720
that technology serves the
public in the way it really

00:40:52.720 --> 00:40:54.920
broadly needs to do.

00:40:54.920 --> 00:40:58.960
This is definitely the spirit
of MIT's College of Computing.

00:40:58.960 --> 00:41:01.030
OK, next question.

00:41:01.030 --> 00:41:03.190
Does Microsoft
have plans to allow

00:41:03.190 --> 00:41:06.190
people to work from home
permanently like Twitter

00:41:06.190 --> 00:41:07.180
recently announced?

00:41:07.180 --> 00:41:09.670
[COMPUTER TONES]

00:41:14.660 --> 00:41:16.260
OK.

00:41:16.260 --> 00:41:20.730
I wonder if we should
wait for a minute

00:41:20.730 --> 00:41:25.020
to see if Brad can rejoin us.

00:41:25.020 --> 00:41:28.710
Otherwise, I hope you've
enjoyed the conversation.

00:41:28.710 --> 00:41:33.450
I certainly have learned
so much from Brad's wisdom.

00:41:33.450 --> 00:41:39.540
I encourage all of
you to read his book.

00:41:39.540 --> 00:41:44.880
It's really a tour de force
through many ethical challenges

00:41:44.880 --> 00:41:52.560
that we as a community face
around the use of technology.

00:41:52.560 --> 00:41:56.530
So let's see.

00:41:56.530 --> 00:42:00.870
There's some questions
posted on Q&amp;A.

00:42:00.870 --> 00:42:09.720
And I am going to wait for just
a minute to see if anybody is--

00:42:09.720 --> 00:42:11.110
oh, look, Brad is back.

00:42:11.110 --> 00:42:12.138
Excellent.

00:42:16.820 --> 00:42:19.596
We are so excited to
see you back, Brad.

00:42:19.596 --> 00:42:20.570
[LAUGHTER]

00:42:20.570 --> 00:42:23.490
I just hope I can stay.

00:42:23.490 --> 00:42:23.990
OK.

00:42:23.990 --> 00:42:27.950
Oh, I asked you a question
that made you run away.

00:42:27.950 --> 00:42:30.020
There's a lot of benefits
from working at home,

00:42:30.020 --> 00:42:34.590
but having your own home
computer managed by the company

00:42:34.590 --> 00:42:36.300
may be one or not.

00:42:36.300 --> 00:42:37.940
It definitely keeps
it more secure

00:42:37.940 --> 00:42:41.760
but it adds this level
of unpredictability.

00:42:41.760 --> 00:42:44.840
Let me just say, at
Microsoft we first

00:42:44.840 --> 00:42:47.750
told people, hey, feel
comfortable working

00:42:47.750 --> 00:42:51.200
from home through October,
until the beginning of November.

00:42:51.200 --> 00:42:54.440
I think your real question is,
what does the future look like

00:42:54.440 --> 00:42:58.040
when COVID-19 is a memory?

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.710
I think we'll have more
people working from home.

00:43:00.710 --> 00:43:03.410
I still think we're
going to have offices.

00:43:03.410 --> 00:43:05.870
You know, Satya Nadella,
our CEO, I think,

00:43:05.870 --> 00:43:08.450
put it really well at a
conversation he had yesterday.

00:43:08.450 --> 00:43:11.390
He said, let's not replace
one dogma with another.

00:43:11.390 --> 00:43:14.840
The old dogma was everybody
needs to be in the office.

00:43:14.840 --> 00:43:16.760
Let's not create
a new dogma that

00:43:16.760 --> 00:43:18.950
says no one should ever
see each other in person,

00:43:18.950 --> 00:43:22.040
we can all do things
through these screens.

00:43:22.040 --> 00:43:24.170
I think that what
we're going to find

00:43:24.170 --> 00:43:26.810
is more choices for more people.

00:43:26.810 --> 00:43:29.330
We'll see the technology
continue to advance.

00:43:29.330 --> 00:43:31.430
Certainly we're
working to accelerate

00:43:31.430 --> 00:43:36.310
the pace of innovation for
our products, like Teams.

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:39.070
But I do think that
at the end of the day,

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:41.110
there is something
important about seeing

00:43:41.110 --> 00:43:42.780
each other in person.

00:43:42.780 --> 00:43:44.770
There is these
conversations that

00:43:44.770 --> 00:43:50.050
happen before a meeting
begins, after a class ends.

00:43:50.050 --> 00:43:52.540
I think there are still
important things that

00:43:52.540 --> 00:43:56.840
happen when people sit down
and have lunch together.

00:43:56.840 --> 00:43:59.400
So we should keep
all of that in mind.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:01.900
I think we're going to live in
a world that has more of what

00:44:01.900 --> 00:44:03.880
we call remote everything.

00:44:03.880 --> 00:44:07.030
There will be more
remote digital services.

00:44:07.030 --> 00:44:08.530
But I think our
ultimate goal should

00:44:08.530 --> 00:44:11.380
be to bring out the
best of what that offers

00:44:11.380 --> 00:44:14.650
and the best of what
we as human beings

00:44:14.650 --> 00:44:18.370
get by being able to spend
time together in person.

00:44:18.370 --> 00:44:20.860
So the next question is
how you envision the world

00:44:20.860 --> 00:44:22.570
in the next 10 to 20 years.

00:44:22.570 --> 00:44:27.160
You touched upon how we're
going to work post-COVID.

00:44:27.160 --> 00:44:32.270
What about living and
playing and learning?

00:44:32.270 --> 00:44:35.350
Well, in addition to working
at Microsoft every day,

00:44:35.350 --> 00:44:36.580
I'm on the board of Netflix.

00:44:36.580 --> 00:44:39.740
So I get to see things from
that perspective as well.

00:44:39.740 --> 00:44:41.530
And let's face it,
this isn't just

00:44:41.530 --> 00:44:43.150
something of the
last couple of months

00:44:43.150 --> 00:44:47.050
where we're all relying
on remote entertainment,

00:44:47.050 --> 00:44:50.290
but we've been seeing this
change over the course

00:44:50.290 --> 00:44:52.560
of a number of years.

00:44:52.560 --> 00:44:55.600
Yeah, clearly the use
of digital technology

00:44:55.600 --> 00:44:59.410
is going to be one of the
defining trends of the 2020s,

00:44:59.410 --> 00:45:02.950
as it really was for the 2010s.

00:45:02.950 --> 00:45:06.220
But what I would say is that
while it has so much potential

00:45:06.220 --> 00:45:09.100
to bring improvements
to people's lives,

00:45:09.100 --> 00:45:12.550
we also do need to think
hard about the inequalities

00:45:12.550 --> 00:45:15.110
that technology can create.

00:45:15.110 --> 00:45:17.260
You know, income
inequality has become

00:45:17.260 --> 00:45:18.910
one of the really
important issues

00:45:18.910 --> 00:45:22.530
in the United States and
many other countries.

00:45:22.530 --> 00:45:24.490
And digital technology,
to some degree,

00:45:24.490 --> 00:45:28.060
has exacerbated that,
in part because we're

00:45:28.060 --> 00:45:32.530
seeing tech companies do
so well, in all honesty.

00:45:32.530 --> 00:45:35.500
Businesses that are not
grounded in technology

00:45:35.500 --> 00:45:37.150
have had a harder time.

00:45:37.150 --> 00:45:39.700
But I think it is
broader than that.

00:45:39.700 --> 00:45:43.000
I think we live in a world
with internet inequality.

00:45:43.000 --> 00:45:45.010
You see this very
sharply when you

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:47.080
see the tens of
millions of Americans

00:45:47.080 --> 00:45:49.390
that don't have
access to broadband.

00:45:49.390 --> 00:45:54.550
I think we live in a world
of educational inequality.

00:45:54.550 --> 00:45:56.890
And digital skills and
the heightened need

00:45:56.890 --> 00:46:01.180
for digital skills is
shining a spotlight on that.

00:46:01.180 --> 00:46:04.000
I think we live in a world
where digital technology can

00:46:04.000 --> 00:46:09.220
do so much but it can exacerbate
the inequalities, for example,

00:46:09.220 --> 00:46:13.030
that people with disabilities
may confront, especially

00:46:13.030 --> 00:46:16.870
if we don't do a great job
of designing technology

00:46:16.870 --> 00:46:19.040
to meet their needs.

00:46:19.040 --> 00:46:22.810
So I think that these
inequalities of the world

00:46:22.810 --> 00:46:26.680
will be one of the important
issues of this decade.

00:46:26.680 --> 00:46:29.020
They're all going to be
more pronounced because

00:46:29.020 --> 00:46:32.920
of this extraordinary
recession, if not depression,

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:35.780
emerging from COVID-19.

00:46:35.780 --> 00:46:38.560
And I think for all
of us who aspire

00:46:38.560 --> 00:46:43.210
to have geeks as the heroes, as
you said, I think we need geeks

00:46:43.210 --> 00:46:46.180
to be heroes and we need
teachers to be heroes

00:46:46.180 --> 00:46:49.480
and we need public
officials to be heroes.

00:46:49.480 --> 00:46:53.950
Because if we're not careful
these advances in technology

00:46:53.950 --> 00:46:58.660
will make the inequalities
greater rather than smaller.

00:46:58.660 --> 00:47:03.300
So let me try to get a few
more audience questions in.

00:47:03.300 --> 00:47:05.130
What do you see as
the role of Microsoft

00:47:05.130 --> 00:47:07.110
in the open source community?

00:47:07.110 --> 00:47:10.872
Well, you know, one of the
more interesting few pages

00:47:10.872 --> 00:47:12.330
in our book, I
would say, certainly

00:47:12.330 --> 00:47:14.980
for those of us who are
involved in experiencing it,

00:47:14.980 --> 00:47:17.410
and for Carol Ann and I
when we wrote about it,

00:47:17.410 --> 00:47:20.790
is we acknowledge very
directly that Microsoft,

00:47:20.790 --> 00:47:23.790
as they say in the book, was
on the wrong side of history

00:47:23.790 --> 00:47:26.790
when open source really
exploded at the beginning

00:47:26.790 --> 00:47:28.290
of this century.

00:47:28.290 --> 00:47:30.360
And I can say that not
just about Microsoft,

00:47:30.360 --> 00:47:33.540
I can say it about
me personally.

00:47:33.540 --> 00:47:36.090
The good news is if
life is long enough

00:47:36.090 --> 00:47:37.920
and you're successful
in other areas,

00:47:37.920 --> 00:47:39.750
you can be on the
wrong side of history

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:42.780
and learn that you're there
and you need to change.

00:47:42.780 --> 00:47:44.790
And that's what we did.

00:47:44.790 --> 00:47:48.270
Today Microsoft is the
single largest contributor

00:47:48.270 --> 00:47:51.210
to open source
projects in the world,

00:47:51.210 --> 00:47:53.430
at least when it
comes to businesses.

00:47:53.430 --> 00:47:55.680
And we're very proud of that.

00:47:55.680 --> 00:48:00.150
When we look at GitHub,
we see GitHub as the home

00:48:00.150 --> 00:48:02.260
for open source development.

00:48:02.260 --> 00:48:05.820
And we have a
responsibility as a steward

00:48:05.820 --> 00:48:10.920
to make it a great and
secure and productive home

00:48:10.920 --> 00:48:13.810
for the world's open
source developers.

00:48:13.810 --> 00:48:16.950
So we're much more now
in the open source camp

00:48:16.950 --> 00:48:21.240
than outside it, even
if not every piece

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:24.660
of code that we write
is developed as part

00:48:24.660 --> 00:48:26.755
of an open source project.

00:48:26.755 --> 00:48:29.130
I think in some ways, one of
the other really interesting

00:48:29.130 --> 00:48:33.840
questions for all of us is how
do we learn from open source

00:48:33.840 --> 00:48:38.340
and apply that learning to what
we now refer to as open data.

00:48:38.340 --> 00:48:40.330
We launched, just
a few weeks ago,

00:48:40.330 --> 00:48:44.550
what we're calling an open
data campaign, a global effort

00:48:44.550 --> 00:48:46.950
that we think, really
needs to be focused

00:48:46.950 --> 00:48:50.530
on ensuring that more data
sets, perhaps especially data

00:48:50.530 --> 00:48:56.190
sets that relate to great
societal issues, are opened up.

00:48:56.190 --> 00:48:58.380
So we've created five
principles that we're

00:48:58.380 --> 00:49:01.710
applying to ourselves and
the data that we have.

00:49:01.710 --> 00:49:05.430
We're investing in more
work with nonprofits.

00:49:05.430 --> 00:49:07.620
We're promoting public policy.

00:49:07.620 --> 00:49:10.530
Ultimately, I think the
great need in the world

00:49:10.530 --> 00:49:13.320
is to ensure that those
that have smaller data

00:49:13.320 --> 00:49:17.640
sets, whether they be companies,
small businesses, universities,

00:49:17.640 --> 00:49:20.580
or the like, have
the opportunity

00:49:20.580 --> 00:49:24.930
to scale by federating their
data with data sets held

00:49:24.930 --> 00:49:26.940
by other institutions.

00:49:26.940 --> 00:49:29.130
But there is a
clear need to do so

00:49:29.130 --> 00:49:31.530
with the kinds of
platform and tools

00:49:31.530 --> 00:49:34.530
that a company like
Microsoft creates.

00:49:34.530 --> 00:49:37.110
It is an imperative
to protect privacy.

00:49:37.110 --> 00:49:40.770
We're very enthused about new
techniques like differential

00:49:40.770 --> 00:49:44.310
privacy and where we see
that technology going.

00:49:44.310 --> 00:49:47.732
There needs to be
the recognition

00:49:47.732 --> 00:49:49.440
that people need to
be able to share data

00:49:49.440 --> 00:49:52.650
without surrendering their
ownership or control of it.

00:49:52.650 --> 00:49:55.210
There's a lot here
that is very broad.

00:49:55.210 --> 00:49:58.230
And I think this is another area
that's really ripe for progress

00:49:58.230 --> 00:49:59.340
at a place like MIT.

00:50:03.280 --> 00:50:05.550
OK, so if you were
a student today,

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:08.910
what would be your major?

00:50:08.910 --> 00:50:11.270
You know, I'd
majored, as I said,

00:50:11.270 --> 00:50:14.030
I'm the liberal arts side of
liberal arts meets engineering

00:50:14.030 --> 00:50:15.830
at Microsoft.

00:50:15.830 --> 00:50:18.650
I like to quote
Steve Jobs, who said

00:50:18.650 --> 00:50:20.750
that he worked every
day at the intersection

00:50:20.750 --> 00:50:23.890
between engineering
and the liberal arts.

00:50:23.890 --> 00:50:26.540
It is a way of reminding
us all that in fact we

00:50:26.540 --> 00:50:30.350
need the kind of
multidisciplinary approach

00:50:30.350 --> 00:50:33.500
that I was talking about before.

00:50:33.500 --> 00:50:37.520
I majored in international
relations at Princeton.

00:50:37.520 --> 00:50:41.430
I'd probably major again,
if I could, in history.

00:50:41.430 --> 00:50:44.780
I have just found it
remarkably helpful for somebody

00:50:44.780 --> 00:50:49.292
who does the work that I do to
be able to learn from the past.

00:50:49.292 --> 00:50:51.500
You know, one of the
interesting things about history

00:50:51.500 --> 00:50:54.042
is that ultimately there's sort
of the history of everything,

00:50:54.042 --> 00:50:55.860
including the
history of science,

00:50:55.860 --> 00:50:57.680
the history of technology.

00:50:57.680 --> 00:51:00.530
I definitely would not be
the kind of student that

00:51:00.530 --> 00:51:03.440
would study only one thing.

00:51:03.440 --> 00:51:05.870
One of the great things you
realize later on in life

00:51:05.870 --> 00:51:07.970
is that when you're
in a university,

00:51:07.970 --> 00:51:12.210
it's an enormous opportunity
to study lots of other things.

00:51:12.210 --> 00:51:16.560
And so that's what I would
encourage people to do.

00:51:16.560 --> 00:51:19.070
Brad, there is a lot of
uncertainty in the world

00:51:19.070 --> 00:51:24.620
right now and a lot of
reasons people feel anxious.

00:51:24.620 --> 00:51:28.210
On the flip side, I'm just
curious what motivates you

00:51:28.210 --> 00:51:33.050
and what makes your clock
tick and get up and open

00:51:33.050 --> 00:51:38.450
the day with an exciting
outlook every morning?

00:51:38.450 --> 00:51:40.760
Well, look, this is a hard time.

00:51:40.760 --> 00:51:42.830
Let's just recognize
the obvious.

00:51:42.830 --> 00:51:45.200
Even for those of us who
have the great good fortune

00:51:45.200 --> 00:51:49.790
to have jobs, to not be among
the 36 million Americans who

00:51:49.790 --> 00:51:51.830
have filed for
unemployment claims

00:51:51.830 --> 00:51:54.830
in the last three months,
to be among the people who

00:51:54.830 --> 00:51:58.640
are healthy, this is
a challenging time.

00:51:58.640 --> 00:52:01.790
But I also think that because
of the challenging times

00:52:01.790 --> 00:52:04.880
that we confront the truth
is the world has never

00:52:04.880 --> 00:52:08.360
needed digital technology
to do as much as it

00:52:08.360 --> 00:52:10.900
needs it to do right now.

00:52:10.900 --> 00:52:13.360
And even when
COVID-19 is a memory--

00:52:13.360 --> 00:52:15.730
it will become a memory--

00:52:15.730 --> 00:52:19.300
you know, we're not going to be
talking about COVID-19 in 2030

00:52:19.300 --> 00:52:23.950
as much as we'll be talking
about carbon, I suspect--

00:52:23.950 --> 00:52:27.100
the world will need the kind
of technology we can create.

00:52:27.100 --> 00:52:29.890
And when I say we, I don't
mean we at Microsoft,

00:52:29.890 --> 00:52:33.250
I mean all of us who are
having this conversation.

00:52:33.250 --> 00:52:37.060
I mean all of you who are
getting a degree at MIT

00:52:37.060 --> 00:52:38.380
right now.

00:52:38.380 --> 00:52:42.950
This is the future that
you're going to create.

00:52:42.950 --> 00:52:45.190
And you have a
bigger opportunity,

00:52:45.190 --> 00:52:48.400
I think, to have a more
important and more positive

00:52:48.400 --> 00:52:54.400
impact in the world than perhaps
any generation that has ever

00:52:54.400 --> 00:52:57.640
studied at MIT has had before.

00:52:57.640 --> 00:53:02.530
Because digital technology is
just ubiquitous in the world.

00:53:02.530 --> 00:53:05.450
And the opportunity to ensure
that it not only works well,

00:53:05.450 --> 00:53:07.720
but it does good for
the world, oh, my

00:53:07.720 --> 00:53:11.085
gosh, how can you not be
excited about getting up

00:53:11.085 --> 00:53:11.710
in the morning.

00:53:11.710 --> 00:53:14.080
That's sort of what I feel.

00:53:14.080 --> 00:53:17.980
And yeah, I just am frankly so
happy I've had the opportunity

00:53:17.980 --> 00:53:21.370
to work with great
scientists and engineers

00:53:21.370 --> 00:53:23.330
on technology that matters.

00:53:23.330 --> 00:53:26.030
And I really hope that
all of you will as well.

00:53:26.030 --> 00:53:30.700
It's something that should
inspire all of us, I believe.

00:53:30.700 --> 00:53:34.670
Well, this is fantastic
advice for our students.

00:53:34.670 --> 00:53:36.220
Thank you for that.

00:53:36.220 --> 00:53:39.310
If you go back 40
years, in hindsight,

00:53:39.310 --> 00:53:42.490
what advice from a
global leader would

00:53:42.490 --> 00:53:46.600
have been most useful
for you at that time?

00:53:46.600 --> 00:53:48.440
You know, I've worked
with Bill Gates,

00:53:48.440 --> 00:53:50.330
Steve Ballmer, Satya Nadella.

00:53:50.330 --> 00:53:54.140
I've had the opportunity to meet
a lot of great world leaders

00:53:54.140 --> 00:53:55.550
in my job.

00:53:55.550 --> 00:53:59.360
The one thing that I find
truly successful people have

00:53:59.360 --> 00:54:03.930
in common is enormous curiosity.

00:54:03.930 --> 00:54:06.560
And I think it's something that
if you're at a place like MIT,

00:54:06.560 --> 00:54:08.630
you probably have already.

00:54:08.630 --> 00:54:11.960
But the advice I would
give is it's like a muscle.

00:54:11.960 --> 00:54:13.250
Exercise it.

00:54:13.250 --> 00:54:14.630
Use it.

00:54:14.630 --> 00:54:18.620
Push yourself to read
more, listen more,

00:54:18.620 --> 00:54:21.770
learn more in more fields.

00:54:21.770 --> 00:54:24.470
Start with what is
at the core of what

00:54:24.470 --> 00:54:28.820
is your major or focus
in graduate education,

00:54:28.820 --> 00:54:31.160
but keep adding to that.

00:54:31.160 --> 00:54:34.530
And what you'll find,
I think, is two things.

00:54:34.530 --> 00:54:37.100
One is there are more dots
that you can connect than you

00:54:37.100 --> 00:54:39.620
can ever would have imagined.

00:54:39.620 --> 00:54:44.240
More things relate to each other
as you broaden the aperture.

00:54:44.240 --> 00:54:48.050
But the second thing is
life is just more fun when

00:54:48.050 --> 00:54:50.960
you ask people questions.

00:54:50.960 --> 00:54:52.880
It's a lot more
interesting when you

00:54:52.880 --> 00:54:55.910
get to learn from other people.

00:54:55.910 --> 00:54:59.520
And develop that
sense of curiosity.

00:54:59.520 --> 00:55:03.380
It's not only something that
will make you more successful,

00:55:03.380 --> 00:55:06.390
I think it will make
your life more enjoyable.

00:55:06.390 --> 00:55:08.900
And I benefited from a
lot of similar advice.

00:55:08.900 --> 00:55:13.040
So I think I heard some of
that when I was a student.

00:55:13.040 --> 00:55:16.280
But if I could go back in time,
I would write a little note

00:55:16.280 --> 00:55:21.780
to myself and I'd probably
read it every morning.

00:55:21.780 --> 00:55:22.940
Well, I'm inspired.

00:55:22.940 --> 00:55:27.230
And unfortunately we are at
the top of the hour with you.

00:55:27.230 --> 00:55:31.780
And we will have to save the
other questions for the future.

00:55:31.780 --> 00:55:33.920
So I would like to
take this opportunity

00:55:33.920 --> 00:55:37.640
to thank you for sharing your
wisdom and insights with us

00:55:37.640 --> 00:55:39.740
and also give you
an open invitation

00:55:39.740 --> 00:55:43.400
to come back for an
in-person visit to our campus

00:55:43.400 --> 00:55:46.340
when we are open
for business again.

00:55:46.340 --> 00:55:48.020
I look forward to
being there in person.

00:55:48.020 --> 00:55:49.940
I always enjoy my visits.

00:55:49.940 --> 00:55:52.490
It's great to connect
digitally, but it's even

00:55:52.490 --> 00:55:54.230
more fun to be there in person.

00:55:54.230 --> 00:55:54.860
So thank you.

00:55:58.340 --> 00:56:00.710
Thank you, Brad, for
joining us today.

00:56:00.710 --> 00:56:03.320
And thank you,
Daniela, for moderating

00:56:03.320 --> 00:56:06.950
this truly fascinating and
really deeply informative

00:56:06.950 --> 00:56:09.470
conversation, which I knew
it would be in advance.

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:11.960
But it's great to see
it actually realized.

00:56:11.960 --> 00:56:16.760
And in fact, even the little
temporary gap in the middle,

00:56:16.760 --> 00:56:19.550
at least for me, it gave me a
little bit of time to catch up,

00:56:19.550 --> 00:56:23.210
because just this
constant sort of pacing

00:56:23.210 --> 00:56:26.020
of questions and answers, I
needed a little processing

00:56:26.020 --> 00:56:26.520
time.

00:56:26.520 --> 00:56:29.840
So it just-- thank you so much.

00:56:29.840 --> 00:56:31.362
Really fantastic.

00:56:31.362 --> 00:56:32.570
Just one housekeeping thing--

00:56:32.570 --> 00:56:35.141
While you were catching
up, I was panicking.

00:56:39.700 --> 00:56:42.950
But it was, you know, it
was a good little interlude

00:56:42.950 --> 00:56:44.960
for the audience--

00:56:44.960 --> 00:56:46.060
Thank you.

00:56:46.060 --> 00:56:48.176
--with 2020 hindsight.

00:56:48.176 --> 00:56:52.960
Just remember, Microsoft,
we make software secure.

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:53.520
Yeah.

00:56:53.520 --> 00:56:54.320
Well.

00:56:54.320 --> 00:56:55.460
But this is the world.

00:56:55.460 --> 00:56:58.710
If we're going to be
more secure, if security

00:56:58.710 --> 00:57:00.580
isn't first, it never happens.

00:57:00.580 --> 00:57:02.050
So it's actually a good lesson.

00:57:02.050 --> 00:57:03.830
That's right.

00:57:03.830 --> 00:57:08.670
We always put it off otherwise.

00:57:08.670 --> 00:57:10.700
But just one housekeeping thing.

00:57:10.700 --> 00:57:12.760
We'll be posting a
video of today's event

00:57:12.760 --> 00:57:16.530
on both the CSAIL and
College of Computing websites

00:57:16.530 --> 00:57:18.990
once we have the time to
get it closed captioned.

00:57:18.990 --> 00:57:20.340
So thank you all.

00:57:20.340 --> 00:57:24.270
Everybody, especially in these
times, take care and be well.

00:57:24.270 --> 00:57:25.950
Thank you for joining us.

00:57:25.950 --> 00:57:26.680
Thank you.

00:57:26.680 --> 00:57:28.470
Thank you.

